00:01:39,813 --> 00:01:42,564
What do I need to remember?

22
00:01:43,100 --> 00:01:50,319
--cpus N sets a hard ceiling of N cores' worth of CPU time (cgroup cpu.max).

23
00:01:50,867 --> 00:01:57,901
A limited container is throttled (slowed down), never killed, for using too much CPU.

24
00:01:58,467 --> 00:02:05,846
--cpuset-cpus pins to cores; --cpu-shares only weights containers against each other under contention.

25
00:02:07,600 --> 00:02:10,743
I keep hearing about memory limits.

26
00:02:10,743 --> 00:02:12,450
What is it, really?

27
00:02:13,000 --> 00:02:15,177
Memory is different from CPU:

28
00:02:15,177 --> 00:02:18,930
CPU can be slowed down, memory cannot be "slowed".

29
00:02:18,930 --> 00:02:22,082
When a container reaches its memory limit,

30
00:02:22,082 --> 00:02:25,986
the kernel first reclaims what it can (file caches),

31
00:02:25,986 --> 00:02:33,567
and if the container still needs more, the kernel's OOM killer (out of memory) kills a process in it.

32
00:02:33,567 --> 00:02:42,949
Without a limit, a leaking container can eat the host's memory until the kernel starts killing processes of other containers,

33
00:02:42,949 --> 00:02:44,075
or of the host.

